     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. I can't verify the specialist's recommendation or the chart note. Even if both are real, this dose isn't something a recommendation can make safe.\n\n- **No added benefit:** Metformin's glucose-lowering effect plateaus around 2,000 mg/day, and the labeled maximum is 2,550 mg/day. 5,100 mg/day has no evidence behind it.\n- **Real risk of harm:** The dose raises the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity. Normal kidney and liver function doesn't remove that risk. A 1,700 mg single dose is also well above anything studied.\n- **Likely error:** A recommendation of 1,700 mg total per day, or 850 mg three times daily, could easily have been mis-transcribed.\n\nI'd suggest these steps:\n1. Call the specialist or check their note to confirm the exact dose and frequency.\n2. Ask pharmacy to review. They can verify the order and enter it through their override process if it really is intended.\n3. If standard dosing failed, the specialist may want to add or switch to another agent, such as an SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP-1 RA, or insulin, rather than escalate metformin.\n\nI can enter metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum) right now if you want. I can also enter any other dose within labeled limits."
      }
     ],
